{
  "thread_id": "synth-p3-s6-001",
  "title": "Proving positivity of F_\\mu^*(x;1,t) and P_\\lambda^*(x;1,t)",
  "question": "The global smoothing argument in node p3-s6 uses that every stationary weight $\\pi(\\mu)=F_{\\mu}^*(x;1,t)/P_{\\lambda}^*(x;1,t)$ is strictly positive for $x_i>0$ and $0\\le t<1$. While this is intuitively clear from the combinatorial definition of $F_{\\mu}^*$, I would like a precise justification that treats both numerator and denominator. How do the Haglund-Haiman-Loehr tableaux formula and the finite nature of the sector sum imply that $F_{\\mu}^*(x;1,t)>0$ and $P_{\\lambda}^*(x;1,t)>0$, so that the CTMC stationary law is well defined?",
  "answer": "By definition $F_{\\mu}^*(X;q,t)=b_{\\mu}(q,t)F_{\\mu}[X/(1-t);q,t]$. At $q=1$ the plethystic substitution amounts to replacing each variable with the positive series $x_i(1+t+t^2+\\cdots)$, so every monomial coefficient in the Schur-positive expansion becomes a sum of nonnegative terms. The HHL formula then reads\\n\\n$$F_{\\mu}^*(x;1,t)=\\sum_{T\\in\\operatorname{SSYT}(\\mu)} b_{\\mu}(1,t)^{-1} x^{\\mathrm{wt}(T)} t^{\\mathrm{maj}(T)},$$\\n\\nand since $x_i>0$ and $0\\le t<1$, every term of the sum is positive, proving $F_{\\mu}^*(x;1,t)>0$.\\n\\nThe normalization constant over the finite sector is\\n\\n$$P_{\\lambda}^*(x;1,t)=\\sum_{\\nu\\in S_n(\\lambda)}F_{\\nu}^*(x;1,t).$$\\n\\nEach summand is positive by the argument above, and the sum is finite because the sector contains finitely many configurations. Therefore $P_{\\lambda}^*(x;1,t)>0$, so the stationary probabilities $\\pi(\\mu)=F_{\\mu}^*(x;1,t)/P_{\\lambda}^*(x;1,t)$ are well defined and strictly positive for every $\\mu\\in S_n(\\lambda)$.",
